# Models (large binary files) — the repo-root GGUF directory only.
# Anchored with a leading slash: an unanchored `models/` matches a directory of that name at
# ANY depth, which silently swallowed site/dev/src/content/docs/models/ (the published
# models documentation) and is why contracts/models/ needed the negations below.
/models/
# ...but contracts/models/ holds the tracked source-of-truth model manifest (no GGUFs).
# Redundant against the anchored pattern above; kept so re-widening it cannot lose them.
!contracts/models/
!contracts/models/*.yaml

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
*.pyd
.Python
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.eggs/

# Virtual environments
.venv*/
venv*/
env/

# Environments
.env
.env.*

# uv
.uv/

# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.ipynb_checkpoints

# Test / coverage
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# Type checking
.mypy_cache/

# Linting
.ruff_cache/

# Artifacts & outputs
notebooks/artifacts/
**/notebooks/artifacts/
*.lock

# Backend benchmark output (machine-specific; curated results live in the plan)
bench_results.csv

# Eval suite — all generated media (fixtures, baselines, model outputs)
sandbox/
src/skills/*/eval/fixtures_cache/

# evals — commit durable summaries; ignore generated media and per-run scratch
evals/**
!evals/
!evals/**/
!evals/INDEX.md
!evals/**/score.json
!evals/**/report.md
!evals/**/review_log.json
# A row-level review and the machine-readable comparison behind a promote/reject decision
# are evidence, not scratch. The 2026-09-17 audit shipped a `report.md` whose verdict —
# reject the candidate — is argued entirely in a sibling `review-v1.md`, with the per-row
# deltas in `comparison.json`; both were ignored, so three committed files linked to a
# document that existed on one machine. A decision nobody can re-read is not reviewable.
# Small (tens of KB) and diffable. Per-arm scoreboards stay ignored; `score.json` above is
# still the conventional summary.
!evals/**/review-*.md
# A promotion/rejection verdict is the decision itself, not a note about it. T7 of the
# reject/clarify plan requires it recorded in the run folder *before* the baseline moves,
# precisely so the comparison cannot be re-derived from the moved baseline afterwards --
# which makes it the one file in the folder that cannot be regenerated.
!evals/**/PROMOTION_VERDICT.md
!evals/**/comparison.json
!evals/**/instrument_comparison.json
# Retrieval baselines are a committed acceptance bar, not scratch — the CI gate
# (test_retrieval_no_regression_vs_baseline, run by `just check`) reads one by path and
# errors outright if it is missing. Same role as skills/*/data/eval_snapshot.json; ~1 KB.
!evals/retrieval/*.json
# Parity baselines are durable comparison artifacts, not scratch — same rationale as the
# retrieval baselines above. A pre-fix run under evals/parity/ is the "before" half of a
# before/after the code change itself destroys: once the fix lands, no amount of care
# reconstructs it. Small and diffable (a corpus run is ~600 KB of JSONL), and each folder
# carries a meta.json pinning git SHA, corpus/model/binary sha256 and the inference backend,
# so a later comparison can prove it is comparing like with like.
# See docs/plans/2026-08-08-native-python-planning-parity.md (P2b).
!evals/parity/**/*.json
!evals/parity/**/*.jsonl
!evals/parity/**/*.txt
!evals/parity/**/*.md
# S3g factorial summaries are evidence, not scratch: accepting a prompt change as the new
# reference (S5) rests on these numbers, and a decision whose evidence lives only on the
# machine that ran it is not reviewable. Small (a few KB); the per-cell scoreboards behind
# them stay ignored. See docs/plans/2026-09-10-skill-quality-lifecycle.md (S3g).
!evals/**/summary.json
!evals/**/summary.md
# Acceptance records are what the native-status gate READS, not scratch: `just check-gate`
# resolves evals/acceptance/<skill>.json by path and derives a skill's status from it, so an
# uncommitted record means CI sees no evidence for any skill and every status collapses to
# `in-progress`. Written by the checks that verify each layer (never by hand), a few KB, and
# diffable — the hashes in them are the whole point, since a changed hash is what marks a
# layer stale. See docs/plans/2026-09-10-skill-quality-lifecycle.md (G2).
!evals/acceptance/*.json
evals/**/.baselines/
evals/**/out.*
evals/**/report.html

# Web — MkDocs build artifacts (removed once the Astro sites land; see
# docs/plans/2026-08-04-website-split.md)
site/site/
site/knaif-site.zip

# Web — Node / pnpm. `pnpm-lock.yaml` is deliberately NOT ignored: `*.lock` above only
# matches a `.lock` suffix, and Amplify's `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` requires the
# lockfile to be committed. Astro's `dist/` output is already covered by the unanchored
# `dist/` rule in the Python section, but it is named here too so removing that rule
# cannot silently start publishing build output.
node_modules/
.astro/
site/*/dist/
# amplify.yml points pnpm's store here (npm_config_store_dir) so the build cache can cover
# it. That is Amplify-only, but anyone reproducing a build locally materializes a
# multi-hundred-MB store in the working tree, and it is not ignored by anything above.
site/.pnpm-store/

# Private / non-public working documents
docs/programs/

# Training subsystem (optional, not shipped) — derived/throwaway artifacts.
# training/.venv is covered by the .venv/ rule above; models/ (GGUFs) by line 2.
training/.venv/
training/output/
training/cache/
# Derived/throwaway training artifacts (regenerable from train.jsonl + scripts).
training/union_chat.jsonl
training/*_chat.jsonl
training/adapters/
training/merged/
# Unsloth writes its compiled-trainer cache to ./unsloth_compiled_cache (default
# leaf name) in the cwd whenever a run imports unsloth without UNSLOTH_COMPILE_LOCATION
# set — so it can land in the repo root. Ignore it wherever it appears.
unsloth_compiled_cache/
# ...but DO keep the committed provenance lock (otherwise eaten by *.lock above).
# Path is `python/training/` since the repo restructure — the old `training/` form silently
# stopped matching, which only shows up on a fresh clone (an already-tracked file ignores
# .gitignore entirely).
!python/training/requirements-train.lock
# The Rust workspace ships a binary (knaif-cli), so commit its lockfile for reproducible
# release builds (unlike the Python library's uv.lock, which stays ignored above).
!Cargo.lock

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# generated per-run outputs of the agent-vs-knaif experiment harness
scripts/agent_vs_knaif/RESULTS.md
scripts/agent_vs_knaif/RESULTS.json

# Rust build artifacts
/target

# Claude Code worktree isolation scratch dir
.claude/worktrees/

# Unsloth writes generated trainer code here (UNSLOTH_COMPILE_LOCATION in the training
# scripts). Machine-generated, rebuilt on demand, and it linted as 2145 errors when it
# briefly looked like repo source.
python/training/cache/
python/training/output/
python/training/adapters/
python/training/merged/
# The chat datasets belong with them: `build_dataset.py` rebuilds one in seconds from
# skills/*/data/train.jsonl. They were listed at line ~129 under the pre-restructure
# `training/` path, which is exactly the silent-stop-matching bug the comment there warns
# about — four of those six entries were re-added above, these two were missed, so
# union_chat.jsonl has been showing up untracked ever since.
#
# Keeping one is worse than not having it: each row bakes in the RETRIEVED system prompt,
# so a dataset built before a prompt.yaml edit trains the next model against a prompt that
# no longer exists. Regenerate, never reuse.
python/training/union_chat.jsonl
python/training/*_chat.jsonl

# Machine-local workbench config: which native build directories to offer in the selector.
# Paths differ per machine and must never be committed (see docs/plans/2026-09-21-skill-prompt-workbench.md).
workbench.local.yaml
